import {
    bool.Bool
    lists.List
    maps.Map
    ranges.Range
    sets.Set
}

/// A collection that answers whether it holds a value: `contains(c, x)`.
/// The std collections implement it:
///
/// - `Set<T>`: whether `x` is a member, by hash lookup.
/// - `Map<K, V>`: whether `x` is a key, by hash lookup. A value is not a key.
/// - `Range<T>`: whether `x` lies between the ends, by two comparisons. An
///   exclusive range leaves out its end, and an unbounded one checks only its
///   start.
/// - `List<T>` and `Vector<T>`: whether an element is `==` to `x`, by a
///   linear scan.
/// - `Bytes`: whether a byte equals `x`, by a linear scan.
/// - `String`, at three type arguments: whether a `String` occurs as a
///   substring, a `Regex` matches anywhere, or a `Codepoint` occurs. A call
///   takes the impl its argument's type chooses.
///
/// `Iter.contains` answers membership under the same name without
/// implementing the interface: it scans any iterable, a lazy pipeline
/// included.
///
/// `List`, `Vector`, `Bytes`, `String` and `Iter` membership is linear in the
/// collection's size, and stops at the first match. For repeated lookups,
/// convert to a `Set` once.
///
/// Elements compare as `==` does: a struct with a hand-written `Equatable` is
/// compared by it, and Float equality is reflexive, so a `NaN` element is
/// found by `NaN` and `0.0` by `-0.0`.
//! assert Set.contains(%[1, 2], 1)
//! assert Map.contains(%{"a" => 1}, "a")
//! assert List.contains([1, 2, 3], 2)
//! assert Range.contains(1..5, 3)
//
pub interface Contains<T> {
    fn contains(c: self, x: T) -> Bool
}
